"Internal Error ()\nciteturn142view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0045782521006186\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0045782521006186: (403) Forbidden\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2203.07404] Respecting causality is all you need for training physics-informed neural networks (https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.07404)\nciteturn142view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.07404\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 183\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Mar 2022]\nL9: # Title:Respecting causality is all you need for training physics-informed neural networks\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Sifan Wang】, 【6†Shyam Sankaran】, 【7†Paris Perdikaris】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Respecting causality is all you need for training physics-informed neural networks, by Sifan Wang and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:While the popularity of physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) is steadily rising, to this date PINNs have not been successful in simulating dynamical systems whose solution exhibits multi-scale, chaotic or turbulent behavior. In this work we attribute this shortcoming to the inability of existing PINNs formulations to respect the spatio-temporal causal structure that is inherent to the evolution of physical systems.\nL17: We argue that this is a fundamental limitation and a key source of error that can ultimately steer PINN models to converge towards erroneous solutions. We address this pathology by proposing a simple re-formulation of PINNs loss functions that can explicitly account for physical causality during model training.\nL18: We demonstrate that this simple modification alone is enough to introduce significant accuracy improvements, as well as a practical quantitative mechanism for assessing the convergence of a PINNs model. We provide state-of-the-art numerical results across a series of benchmarks for which existing PINNs formulations fail, including the chaotic Lorenz system, the Kuramoto-Sivashinsky equation in the chaotic regime, and the Navier-Stokes equations in the turbulent regime.\nL19: To the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that PINNs have been successful in simulating such systems, introducing new opportunities for their applicability to problems of industrial complexity.\nL20: Comments:  | 35 pages, 27 figures, 4 tables\nL21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Numerical Analysis (math.NA); Chaotic Dynamics (nlin.CD); Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:2203.07404】 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or 【11†arXiv:2203.07404v1】 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2203.07404†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Sifan Wang [【13†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 14 Mar 2022 18:08:18 UTC (8,992 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Respecting causality is all you need for training physics-informed neural networks, by Sifan Wang and 2 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL39: \nL40: 【15†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * 【16†Audio Summary】\nL45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.LG\nL48: \nL49: 【17†< prev】   |   【18†next >】\nL50: \nL51: 【19†new】 | 【20†recent】 | 【21†2022-03】\nL52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\n