nL133: # Demos\nL134: \nL135: [Input] Replicate Toggle\nL136: \nL137: Replicate (【47†What is Replicate?†replicate.com】)\nL138: \nL139: [Input] Spaces Toggle\nL140: \nL141: Hugging Face Spaces (【48†What is Spaces?†huggingface.co】)\nL142: \nL143: [Input] Spaces Toggle\nL144: \nL145: TXYZ.AI (【49†What is TXYZ.AI?†txyz.ai】)\nL146: \nL147: [Input] Related Papers\nL148: # Recommenders and Search Tools\nL149: \nL150: [Input] Link to Influence Flower\nL151: \nL152: Influence Flower (【50†What are Influence Flowers?†influencemap.cmlab.dev】)\nL153: \nL154: [Input] Core recommender toggle\nL155: \nL156: CORE Recommender (【51†What is CORE?†core.ac.uk】)\nL157: \nL158: [Input] IArxiv recommender toggle\nL159: \nL160: IArxiv Recommender (【52†What is IArxiv?†iarxiv.org】)\nL161: \nL162:   * Author\nL163:   * Venue\nL164:   * Institution\nL165:   * Topic\nL166: \nL167: [Input] About arXivLabs\nL168: # arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\nL169: \nL170: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL171: \nL172: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL173: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? 【53†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org】.\nL174: \nL175: 【54†Which authors of this paper are endorsers?】 | Disable MathJax (【55†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL176: \nL177: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, 【56†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\nL178: 【57†About†info.arxiv.org】 · 【58†Help†info.arxiv.org】 · 【59†Contact†info.arxiv.org】 · 【60†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org】 · 【61†Copyright†info.arxiv.org】 · 【62†Privacy†info.arxiv.org】 · 【63†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org】 · 【64†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\nL179: \nL180: Major funding support from\nL181: \nL182: 【65†(opens in new tab)†www.simonsfoundation.org】 【66†(opens in new tab)†www.sfi.org.bm】 【67†(opens in new tab)†www.schmidtsciences.org】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.07404)\nciteturn142view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 35; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.07404\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2275\nL0@P0: RESPECTING CAUSALITY IS ALL YOU NEED FOR TRAINING\nL1@P0: PHYSICS-INFORMED NEURAL NETWORKS\nL2@P0: Sifan Wang\nL3@P0: Graduate Group in Applied Mathematics\nL4@P0: and Computational Science\nL5@P0: University of Pennsylvania\nL6@P0: Philadelphia, PA 19104\nL7@P0: sifanw@sas.upenn.edu\nL8@P0: Shyam Sankaran\nL9@P0: Department of Mechanical Engineering\nL10@P0: and Applied Mechanics\nL11@P0: University of Pennsylvania\nL12@P0: Philadelphia, PA 19104\nL13@P0: shyamss@seas.upenn.edu\nL14@P0: Paris Perdikaris\nL15@P0: Department of Mechanical Engineering\nL16@P0: and Applied Mechanics\nL17@P0: University of Pennsylvania\nL18@P0: Philadelphia, PA 19104\nL19@P0: pgp@seas.upenn.edu\nL20@P0: March 16, 2022\nL21@P0: ABSTRACT\nL22@P0: While the popularity of physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) is steadily rising, to this date\nL23@P0: PINNs have not been successful in simulating dynamical systems whose solution exhibits multi-scale,\nL24@P0: chaotic or turbulent behavior. In this work we attribute this shortcoming to the inability of existing\nL25@P0: PINNs formulations to respect the spatio-temporal causal structure that is inherent to the evolution of\nL26@P0: physical systems. We argue that this is a fundamental limitation and a key source of error that can\nL27@P0: ultimately steer PINN models to converge towards erroneous solutions. We address this pathology by\nL28@P0: proposing a simple re-formulation of PINNs loss functions that can explicitly account for physical\nL29@P0: causality during model training. We demonstrate that this simple modification alone is enough\nL30@P0: to introduce significant accuracy improvements, as well as a practical quantitative mechanism for\nL31@P0: assessing the convergence of a PINNs model. We provide state-of-the-art numerical results across a\nL32@P0: series of benchmarks for which existing PINNs formulations fail, including the chaotic Lorenz system,\nL33@P0: the Kuramoto–Sivashinsky equation in the chaotic regime, and the Navier-Stokes equations in the\nL34@P0: turbulent regime. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that PINNs have been successful\nL35@P0: in simulating such systems, introducing new opportunities for their applicability to problems of\nL36@P0: industrial complexity.\nL37@P0: Keywords Deep learning · Partial differential equations · Computational physics · Chaotic systems\nL38@P0: 1 Introduction\nL39@P0: Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) have emerged as a promising framework for synthesizing observational data\nL40@P0: and physical laws across diverse applications in science and engineering [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]. However, it is well known\nL41@P0: that PINNs often face severe difficulties and even fail to tackle problems whose solution exhibits highly nonlinear,\nL42@P0: multi-scale, or chaotic behavior [9, 10]. Over the last few years, a series of extensions to the original formulation\nL43@P0: of Raissi et al. [11] have been proposed with the sole goal of enhancing the accuracy and robustness of PINNs in\nL44@P0: tackling increasingly more challenging problems. Such extensions include, but are not limited to, novel optimization\nL45@P0: algorithms for adaptive training [12, 13, 14, 15], adaptive algorithms for selecting batches of training data [16, 17],\nL46@P0: novel network architectures [12, 9, 18, 19, 20], domain decomposition strategies [21, 22], new types of activation\nL47@P0-1: arXiv:2203.07404v1 [cs.LG] 14 Mar 2022A PREPRINT - MARCH 16, 2022\nL48@P1: functions [23], and sequential learning strategies [16, 24, 25]. Although these techniques have been successful in\nL49@P1: introducing some improvements in terms of trainability and accuracy, there still exists a vast suite of problems that\nL50@P1: remain elusive to PINNs. Examples of such problems include systems whose behavior exhibits strong non-linearity,\nL51@P1: broadband energy spectra, and high sensitivity to initial conditions, such as the chaotic Kuramoto-Sivishinski equation\nL52@P1: and the Navier-Stokes equations in the turbulent regime. These are not pathological corner cases, but cases that are\nL53@P1: extremely relevant across a multitude of realistic scenarios in science and engineering. Therefore, there is a pressing\nL54@P1: need for understanding why PINNs fall short in such scenarios, and how they can be improved in order to overcome the\nL55@P1: challenges that currently limit their success to relatively simple problems.\nL56@P1: Physical systems are known to possess an inherent causal structure. Consider for example a linear wave with some\nL57@P1: initial velocity that is spreading out with a speed c across a homogeneous medium [26]. It is well-understood that,\nL58@P1: although a part of the wave may lag behind (if there is an initial velocity), no part can travel faster than speed c. This\nL59@P1: assertion encapsulates the so-called principle of causality that dictates how local changes in the initial/boundary data of\nL60@P1: a spatio-temporal dynamical system is reflected in its corresponding states at later times [26]. Specific to hyperbolic\nL61@P1: partial differential equations (PDEs), such as the wave equation, this principle underpins the formulation of the method\nL62@P1: of characteristics [27] that provides a rigorous set of analytical and numerical tools for efficiently tackling initial value\nL63@P1: problems. Although characterizing how information propagates in general nonlinear PDEs is a challenging task, basic\nL64@P1: principles of causality such as temporal precedence and covariation (i.e. statistical dependency between variables that\nL65@P1: are generated by coupled time evolution) are still expected to hold. This causal structure is also clearly reflected in\nL66@P1: classical numerical methods, where a PDE is typically discretized in time by sequential algorithms which ensure that\nL67@P1: the solution at time t is fully resolved before approximating the solution at time t + ∆t. Strikingly, this notion of\nL68@P1: temporal dependence is absent in most continuous-time PINNs formulations (see e.g. [28, 29, 30, 21, 12, 13, 23]).\nL69@P1: In fact, as we will see in section 3, continuous-time PINNs trained by gradient descent are implicitly biased towards\nL70@P1: first approximating PDE solutions at later times, before even resolving the initial conditions, therefore profoundly\nL71@P1: violating temporal causality. Consequently, it is no surprise that such formulations are fragile and often fail to simulate\nL72@P1: forward problems, especially in cases where the target solutions exhibit strong dependence on initial data (e.g. chaotic\nL73@P1: systems). Recent studies [16, 24, 25] have proposed remedies to this issue by empirically introducing sequential training\nL74@P1: strategies, yet a concrete justification of why such strategies appear to be effective is still missing.\nL75@P1: This work is focused on investigating the importance of respecting physical causality during the training of continuous\u0002time PINNs. Our specific contributions can be summarized as:\nL76@P1: • We reveal an implicit bias suggesting that continuous-time PINNs models can violate causality, and hence are\nL77@P1: susceptible to converge towards erroneous solutions.\nL78@P1: • We put forth a simple re-formulation of PINNs loss functions that allows us to explicitly respect the causal\nL79@P1: structure that characterizes the solution of general nonlinear PDEs.\nL80@P1: • Strikingly, we demonstrate that this simple modification alone is enough to introduce significant accuracy\nL81@P1: improvements, allowing us to tackle problems that have remained elusive to PINNs.\nL82@P1: • We provide a practical quantitative criterion for assessing the training convergence of a PINNs model.\nL83@P1: • We examine a collection of challenging benchmarks for which existing PINNs formulations fail, and demon\u0002strate that the proposed causal training strategy leads to state-of-the-art results.\nL84@P1: To the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that PINNs have been successful in simulating systems such as the\nL85@P1: chaotic Lorenz system, the Kuramoto–Sivashinsky equation in the chaotic regime, and the Navier-Stokes equations in\nL86@P1: the turbulent regime, introducing new opportunities for their applicability to problems of industrial complexity.\nL87@P1: The paper is structured as follows. In section 2, we provide an overview of PINNs following the original formulation of\nL88@P1: Raissi et. al. [11]. Using a simple case study, we reveal an implicit bias of continuous-time PINNs that makes them\nL89@P1: prone to violating physical causality, and thereby steering them towards erroneous solutions. To address this drawback,\nL90@P1: in section 3 we put forth a simple re-formulation of the PINNs residual loss and propose a general casual training\nL91@P1: algorithm for explicitly respecting physical causality during model training. Section 4 discusses practical considerations\nL92@P1: specific to enhancing the accuracy and efficiency of PINNs. These developments are put to test in section 5, where we\nL93@P1: demonstrate state-of-the-art results across a comprehensive collection of challenging benchmarks for which existing\nL94@P1: PINN formulations are known to fail. Finally, section 6 provides a summary of our main findings, and touches upon\nL95@P1: remaining limitations and areas for future research.\nL96@P1-2: 2A PREPRINT - MARCH 16, 2022\nL97@P2: 2 Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs)\nL98@P2: Problem setup: We begin with a brief overview of physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) [11] in the context of\nL99@P2: inferring the solutions of PDEs. Generally, we consider PDEs taking the form\nL100@P2: ut + N [u] = 0, t ∈ [0, T], x ∈ Ω, (2.1)\nL101@P2: subject to the initial and boundary conditions\nL102@P2: u(0, x) = g(x), x ∈ Ω, (2.2)\nL103@P2: B[u] = 0, t ∈ [0, T], x ∈ ∂Ω, (2.3)\nL104@P2: where N [·] is a linear or nonlinear differential operator, and B[·] is a boundary operator corresponding to Dirichlet,\nL105@P2: Neumann, Robin, or periodic boundary conditions. In addition, u describes the unknown latent solution that is governed\nL106@P2: by the PDE system of Equation 2.1.\nL107@P2: Following the original work of Raissi et al. [11], we proceed by representing the unknown solution u(t, x) by a deep\nL108@P2: neural network uθ(t, x), where θ denotes all tunable parameters of the network (e.g., weights and biases). Then, a\nL109@P2: physics-informed model can be trained by minimizing the following composite loss function\nL110@P2: L(θ) = λicLic(θ) + λbcLbc(θ) + λrLr(θ), (2.4)\nL111@P2: where\nL112@P2: Lic(θ) = 1\nL113@P2: Nic\nL114@P2: X\nL115@P2: Nic\nL116@P2: i=1\nL117@P2: \nL118@P2: \nL119@P2: \nL120@P2: uθ(0, x\nL121@P2: i\nL122@P2: ic) − g(x\nL123@P2: i\nL124@P2: ic)\nL125@P2: \nL126@P2: \nL127@P2: \nL128@P2: \nL129@P2: 2\nL130@P2: , (2.5)\nL131@P2: Lbc(θ) = 1\nL132@P2: Nbc\nL133@P2: X\nL134@P2: Nbc\nL135@P2: i=1\nL136@P2: \nL137@P2: \nL138@P2: \nL139@P2: B[uθ](t\nL140@P2: i\nL141@P2: bc, x\nL142@P2: i\nL143@P2: bc)\nL144@P2: \nL145@P2: \nL146@P2: \nL147@P2: \nL148@P2: 2\nL149@P2: , (2.6)\nL150@P2: Lr(θ) = 1\nL151@P2: Nr\nL152@P2: X\nL153@P2: Nr\nL154@P2: i=1\nL155@P2: \nL156@P2: \nL157@P2: \nL158@P2: \nL159@P2: \nL160@P2: \nL161@P2: \nL162@P2: \nL163@P2: ∂uθ\nL164@P2: ∂t (t\nL165@P2: i\nL166@P2: r\nL167@P2: , x\nL168@P2: i\nL169@P2: r\nL170@P2: ) + N [uθ](t\nL171@P2: i\nL172@P2: r\nL173@P2: , x\nL174@P2: i\nL175@P2: r\nL176@P2: )\nL177@P2: \nL178@P2: \nL179@P2: \nL180@P2: \nL181@P2: \nL182@P2: \nL183@P2: \nL184@P2: \nL185@P2: 2\nL186@P2: . (2.7)\nL187@P2: Here {x\nL188@P2: i\nL189@P2: ic}\nL190@P2: Nic\nL191@P2: i=1, {t\nL192@P2: i\nL193@P2: bc, x\nL194@P2: i\nL195@P2: bc}\nL196@P2: Nbc\nL197@P2: i=1 and {t\nL198@P2: i\nL199@P2: r\nL200@P2: , x\nL201@P2: i\nL202@P2: r}\nL203@P2: Nr\nL204@P2: i=1 can be the vertices of a fixed mesh or points that are randomly sampled\nL205@P2: at each iteration of a gradient descent algorithm. Notice that all required gradients with respect to input variables or\nL206@P2: network parameters θ can be efficiently computed via automatic differentiation [31]. Moreover, the hyper-parameters\nL207@P2: {λic, λbc, λr} allow the flexibility of assigning a different learning rate to each individual loss term in order to balance\nL208@P2: their interplay during model training. These weights may be user-specified or tuned automatically during training\nL209@P2: [12, 13].\nL210@P2: An illustrative example: To motivate the proposed methods described in section 3, let us study a representative case\nL211@P2: with which conventional PINN models are known to struggle. To this end, consider the one-dimensional Allen-Cahn\nL212@P2: equation\nL213@P2: ut − 0.0001uxx + 5u\nL214@P2: 3 − 5u = 0, t ∈ [0, 1], x ∈ [−1, 1], (2.8)\nL215@P2: u(x, 0) = x\nL216@P2: 2\nL217@P2: cos(πx), (2.9)\nL218@P2: u(t, −1) = u(t, 1), (2.10)\nL219@P2: ux(t, −1) = ux(t, 1). (2.11)\nL220@P2: This example is difficult to directly solve with the original continuous-time formulation of Raissi et al. [11], and\nL221@P2: has been recently studied by Wight et. al. [16] and McClenny et. al. [14] who developed adaptive re-sampling and\nL222@P2: weighting algorithms, respectively, to improve the PINNs prediction.\nL223@P2: Following the setup discussed in these studies [14, 16], we represent the latent variable u by a fully-connected neural\nL224@P2: network uθ with tanh activation function, 4 hidden layers and 128 neurons per hidden layer. To further simplify\nL225@P2: the training objective 2.4, we also strictly impose the periodic BCs by embedding the input coordinates into Fourier\nL226@P2: expansion using Equation 4.8 with m = 10 (see section 4 for further details). Then the loss function 2.4 can be reduced\nL227@P2: to\nL228@P2: L(θ) = λicLic(θ) + λrLr(θ), (2.12)\nL229@P2: where Lic(θ) and Lr(θ) are defined exactly the same as in Equation 2.5 and Equation 2.7. For simplicity, we create a\nL230@P2: uniform mesh of size 100 × 256 in the computational domain [0, 1] × [−1, 1], yielding Nic = 256 initial points and\nL231@P2-3: 3A PREPRINT - MARCH 16, 2022\nL232@P3: Nr = 25600 collocation points for enforcing the PDE residual. We also choose λic = 100, λr = 1 for better enforcing\nL233@P3: the initial condition.\nL234@P3: We proceed by training the resulting PINN model via full-batch gradient descent using the Adam optimizer [32] for\nL235@P3: 2 × 105iterations. As shown in Figure 1, even when the periodic boundary conditions are enforced exactly, our\nL236@P3: conventional PINN model is unable to learn the accurate solution for this example. One can also observe that the\nL237@P3: predicted solution seems to get stuck at some intermediate state and cannot be further refined to provide an accurate\nL238@P3: approximation to the ground truth. This is consistent with the left panel of Figure 2 where the loss functions rapidly\nL239@P3: decrease in the first few thousand training iterations, and then barely change for the rest of training, implying that the\nL240@P3: neural network gets trapped in an erroneous local minimum. Unfortunately, such problematic behavior is not a rare\nL241@P3: event, but rather a common outcome for PINNs, especially when solving transient problems [13, 24].\nL242@P3: PINNs can violate physical causality: To explore the underlying reasons behind this failed case study, let us closely\nL243@P3: examine the definition of the residual loss Lr. Before doing so, we will slightly change our notation for convenience.\nL244@P3: Suppose that 0 = t1 < t2 < · · · < tNt = T discretizes the temporal domain, and {xj}\nL245@P3: Nx\nL246@P3: j=1 discretizes the spatial\nL247@P3: domain Ω. For this example, {ti}\nL248@P3: Nt\nL249@P3: i=1 and {xj}\nL250@P3: Nx\nL251@P3: j=1 are uniformly spaced meshes in [0, 1] and [−1, 1], respectively. Now\nL252@P3: for a given spatial discretization {xj}\nL253@P3: Nx\nL254@P3: j=1, we define the temporal residual loss as\nL255@P3: Lr(t, θ) = 1\nL256@P3: Nx\nL257@P3: X\nL258@P3: Nx\nL259@P3: j=1\nL260@P3: |\nL261@P3: ∂uθ\nL262@P3: ∂t (t, xj ) + N [uθ](t, xj )|\nL263@P3: 2\nL264@P3: . (2.13)\nL265@P3: Then, the residual loss 2.7 can be rewritten as\nL266@P3: Lr(θ) = 1\nL267@P3: Nt\nL268@P3: X\nL269@P3: Nt\nL270@P3: i=1\nL271@P3: Lr(ti, θ) (2.14)\nL272@P3: =\nL273@P3: 1\nL274@P3: NtNx\nL275@P3: X\nL276@P3: Nt\nL277@P3: i=1\nL278@P3: X\nL279@P3: Nx\nL280@P3: j=1\nL281@P3: |\nL282@P3: ∂uθ\nL283@P3: ∂t (ti\nL284@P3: , xj ) + N [uθ](ti, xj )|\nL285@P3: 2\nL286@P3: . (2.15)\nL287@P3: Next, we discretize ∂uθ\nL288@P3: ∂t using the forward Euler scheme [33]. For any 1 ≤ i ≤ Nt − 1, L(ti\nL289@P3: , θ) can be approximated\nL290@P3: by\nL291@P3: Lr(ti, θ) ≈\nL292@P3: 1\nL293@P3: Nx\nL294@P3: X\nL295@P3: Nx\nL296@P3: j=1\nL297@P3: \nL298@P3: \nL299@P3: \nL300@P3: \nL301@P3: \nL302@P3: \nL303@P3: \nL304@P3: \nL305@P3: uθ(ti, xj ) − uθ(ti−1, xj )\nL306@P3: ∆t\nL307@P3: + N [uθ](ti, xj )\nL308@P3: \nL309@P3: \nL310@P3: \nL311@P3: \nL312@P3: \nL313@P3: \nL314@P3: \nL315@P3: \nL316@P3: 2\nL317@P3: ≈\nL318@P3: |Ω|\nL319@P3: ∆t\nL320@P3: 2\nL321@P3: Z\nL322@P3: Ω\n"
